00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:14.149
Autumn Routt: So let's talk about inverse kinematics. Why do they matter?

17
00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:22.639
Autumn Routt: Inverse kinematics is a technique that is foundational to all motion capture software, You use inverse kinematics.

18
00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:46.600
Autumn Routt: Any time that you want to figure out what joint angles are with having limited information. So if we need to estimate the angles of joints that we cannot directly put markers onto, then inverse kinematics is an excellent tool. So that's joint centers that don't have a bony landmark, like in humans, the hip, and a lot of animal models, both because of internal joints and because it's just difficult to put markers onto animals.

19
00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:56.960
Autumn Routt: Motion capture relies on inverse kinematics, and any kind of markerless motion capture system is doing inverse kinematics under the hood to make sure that the estimates it's giving you are realistic.

20
00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:10.710
Autumn Routt: So, if we have all of the markers on all of the joints that we want to see, we can still use inverse kinematics as a means of validating the accuracy of the joint data from marker data.

21
00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:21.459
Autumn Routt: We also use it when we want to fill in gaps, so if a marker is occluded, then we can estimate from the markers that we still have remaining on other joints what the angle of that joint should be using this technique.

22
00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:43.890
Autumn Routt: And lastly, if you're making a computational model of movement and you don't want to use any experimental data as training data, then to make that model realistic, you can use inverse kinematics. And then you only need to know the segment lengths of the different joints and where you're aiming for a specific target point on the body to be. And you can estimate various combinations of joint angles that would get that point on the body.

23
00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:49.499
Autumn Routt: to where you need it to be, and that's very helpful in making your model actually be realistic and informative.

