00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:21.869
Autumn Routt: Alright, and let's move on to inverse dynamics. So, inverse dynamics is finding joint moments using kinematics, so that joint angle information we just got with inverse kinematics, and forces.

73
00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:36.679
Autumn Routt: Inverse dynamics is kind of at the heart of all of the joint moment calculations, so if you're using a software that tells you joint moments, which… joint torques, etc, it's probably doing inverse dynamics in a motion capture space.

74
00:24:36.910 --> 00:24:52.249
Autumn Routt: So there are two different ways that we can calculate joint moments using inverse dynamics. The first is a bottom-up approach. So that's where we say we know the ground reaction forces, the GRFs, that's just the force that we can measure with a force plate underneath somebody's feet.

75
00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:59.930
Autumn Routt: And we could use those ground reaction forces and find their distance from the different joints that we're interested in.

76
00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:08.609
Autumn Routt: That knowledge, along with the knowledge of the mass of the segments and the angles of the joints, lets us work from the bottom up to figure out what the joint moments are across the whole system.

77
00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:26.359
Autumn Routt: Or we could do a top-down approach. This is where we say, I'm going to go somewhere in the body where I know the external force, and using just that external force instead of a ground reaction force, I can also work towards the middle of the person, or towards the bottom of the person.

78
00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:50.810
Autumn Routt: And find their joint moments, so the most common approach for the top-down strategy is to start at the head and assume that there is no external force on the head, nobody's pressing down on their head. So you can just say, if I know the mass of their head and of all of their other segments, I should be able to figure out the moments about their joints. Another way that this approach is used is when a leg is in swing phase during walking.

79
00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:57.339
Autumn Routt: Because you know then that that leg has no forces on it besides the ones generated from the mass of its segment alone.

80
00:25:58.040 --> 00:26:03.849
Autumn Routt: So, if anyone here has ever used OpenSim to find joint moments, they're doing

81
00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:09.440
Autumn Routt: Both bottom-up and top-down inverse dynamics, and comparing the results of the two.

82
00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:29.309
Autumn Routt: In a perfect world where there is no error, those are exactly the same. But because we're using devices and strategies that introduce various amounts of error, there's going to often be some amount of difference in the joint moments calculated with bottom up versus top down. And just minimizing that difference as much as possible is how we ensure that our data is accurate.

