242
00:45:48.800 --> 00:46:04.969
Emily Smith: So motion capture isn't a single measurement. It's a sequence of processing steps. In marker-based motion capture, each camera emits infrared light and records the light reflected back from the passive reflective markers.

243
00:46:05.050 --> 00:46:20.200
Emily Smith: A single camera can only determine where a marker is located in its two-dimensional image, but by combining views from multiple calibrated cameras, software triangulates a marker's three-dimensional position.

244
00:46:21.130 --> 00:46:23.549
Emily Smith: Markers are placed on the sub… oh, nope.

245
00:46:24.110 --> 00:46:34.219
Emily Smith: Markers are placed on the subject, cameras detect them, and their three-dimensional positions are reconstructed, and those trajectories are used to build a biomechanical model.

246
00:46:34.330 --> 00:46:39.760
Emily Smith: And finally, we can calculate biomechanical variables from those.

247
00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:57.029
Emily Smith: Markerless motion capture uses one or more synchronized cameras and computer vision algorithms, typically neural networks, to identify anatomical landmarks directly from video instead of using tracking reflective markers.

248
00:46:57.110 --> 00:47:15.719
Emily Smith: Those systems are particularly useful when markers aren't feasible, such as with very small animals and species that don't tolerate markers well, or behaviors where markers would interfere with easily or be easily occluded by the movement you're studying.

249
00:47:16.880 --> 00:47:29.159
Emily Smith: Error can be introduced at every stage, and this error accumulates as you move through this pipeline. We'll break down how error creeps in and what you can do to mitigate it in the next slides.

250
00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:44.339
Emily Smith: Before you ever place a marker, think about the question you are trying to answer. A marker to set design for one application may not be appropriate for another.

251
00:47:44.470 --> 00:47:51.490
Emily Smith: Choose the simplest marker that captures the variables you actually need with the resources that you have.

252
00:47:51.840 --> 00:48:00.509
Emily Smith: Even if you're inheriting a well-studied approach, I encourage you to be thoughtful about why you are choosing the set that you are.

253
00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:18.379
Emily Smith: So your lab may have this down to a T about how you collect, but I think it's almost easier to get lost in the sauce when you're when you're trying to process the data on your own. If you don't investigate thoroughly why the people before you made the choices they did.

254
00:48:18.380 --> 00:48:26.510
Emily Smith: And then if you're lucky, like me, where you're having to develop a new set for a new study, you need to…

255
00:48:27.380 --> 00:48:39.800
Emily Smith: really question, well, do I really need that extra marker? Because that extra marker introduces error and may or may not create problems from you down the road.

