Emily Smith: Next slide.

257
00:48:45.200 --> 00:48:49.120
Emily Smith: Many of the biggest sources of error are preventable.

258
00:48:49.340 --> 00:48:56.910
Emily Smith: We place markers on bony landmarks to minimize soft tissue artifact.

259
00:48:57.020 --> 00:49:13.150
Emily Smith: So the cameras are tracking the markers, not the bones. If a marker is attached to skin over muscle or other tissue, it can move relative to the underlying bone as the muscles contract or the skin shifts.

260
00:49:13.420 --> 00:49:21.930
Emily Smith: The software interprets that marker movement as bone movement, and that introduces error into estimated.

261
00:49:22.510 --> 00:49:23.600
Emily Smith: angles.

262
00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:25.940
Emily Smith: or twerk.

263
00:49:26.860 --> 00:49:46.669
Emily Smith: Use tight-fitting clothing, place markers consistently on anatomical landmarks, remove reflective objects, secure your clusters, and calibrate appropriately. I've now collected data in four different labs. Nope.

264
00:49:46.710 --> 00:50:01.340
Emily Smith: That's not including my undergrad. Five different labs, and they all have a unique calibration system. Do not assume that one way you calibrate in one lab works in every other lab. Also, learn that the hard way.

