375
01:04:22.400 --> 01:04:37.290
Lauren Infantino: So, I think the first part, that we can discuss when we're talking about EMGs is really what information they give you. And all animals, all organisms move through muscular action or contraction.

376
01:04:37.290 --> 01:05:00.609
Lauren Infantino: And this is really what we're investigating when we're using EMGs. We're looking at the coordinated action of muscle groups and how animals move and the control of that movement. And so that's kind of the information that we're going to be getting when we're using surface EMGs, intramuscular EMGs, high density arrays is we're really getting at

377
01:05:00.610 --> 01:05:02.690
Lauren Infantino: How do animals control their movement?

378
01:05:02.810 --> 01:05:15.050
Lauren Infantino: So the information that is mainly going to be encased in our EMG signal is information about the timing, the magnitude, and the duration of muscular action.

379
01:05:15.330 --> 01:05:31.720
Lauren Infantino: And so, for the rest of this, I'll usually just say EMGs, but that's in reference to electromyography. So, all of these animals are moving through contracting their muscles, and that's happening from the central nervous system.

380
01:05:31.720 --> 01:05:48.200
Lauren Infantino: sending action potentials to our muscles, our muscles are contracting, and they're generating work and movement. So just remember that as we kind of continue on, is the central questions that you can answer with EMG are basically regarding how animals move.

381
01:05:48.480 --> 01:06:05.559
Lauren Infantino: So we'll talk about a little bit about what are the underlying principles of how EMGs work, particularly for a bipolar electrode. But we'll kind of start a little bit broader, going back to maybe some things that you've learned out in week one.

382
01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:18.869
Lauren Infantino: really the action potential alpha motor neurons and muscle fibers. So we'll start here with our spinal cord. Spinal cord contains the motor neuron cell body.

383
01:06:19.060 --> 01:06:23.139
Lauren Infantino: of our alpha motor neurons which innervate our muscle fibers.

384
01:06:23.760 --> 01:06:34.019
Lauren Infantino: These motor neurons, these cell bodies, will receive both excitatory signals and inhibitory signals from interneurons and other cells at their dendrites.

385
01:06:34.520 --> 01:06:45.370
Lauren Infantino: The sum of those signals can reach a threshold in which an action potential will be sent along this motor neuron to the neuromuscular junction of a single muscle fiber.

386
01:06:45.440 --> 01:06:50.990
Lauren Infantino: At that junction acetylcholine is usually released, which is the chemical transmitter.

387
01:06:51.010 --> 01:07:03.340
Lauren Infantino: that changes the membrane permeability of our muscle fibers, which triggers an action potential with the influx of sodium into our cells, release of calcium and the contraction of muscles.

388
01:07:03.340 --> 01:07:11.069
Lauren Infantino: So just kind of remember a little bit of that background that we learned from week one, because it'll be important in describing how electromyography works.

389
01:07:12.430 --> 01:07:13.390
Lauren Infantino: So…

390
01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:24.620
Lauren Infantino: If you're going to take away anything from this, it's really what a bipolar, how a bipolar electrode works, which is through detecting the differences in voltages between 2 points.

391
01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:39.300
Lauren Infantino: So we have our action potential that has gone. It's synapsed at a neuromuscular junction with our muscle fiber. And now what we have is an action potential that is propagating in either direction. But in this example it's propagating to the right.

392
01:07:39.890 --> 01:07:49.659
Lauren Infantino: Let's take the scenario where we have an electrode one on some point on our muscle fiber, and electrode 2 is some distance away from it.

393
01:07:49.950 --> 01:07:56.360
Lauren Infantino: As our action potential propagates to the right of our fiber, it'll pass under electrode. One electrode, one will read it.

394
01:07:56.530 --> 01:08:05.280
Lauren Infantino: And then at another point in time, based on the conduction velocity of that action potential through this fiber, it'll pass under electrode 2. Electrode 2 will read it.

395
01:08:05.630 --> 01:08:21.699
Lauren Infantino: for bipolar electrodes. What they're really doing is they're going to take the difference between those 2 voltages or electrodes at a given instant in time. And really, that's what you get at the end of the day is your baseline waveform.

396
01:08:22.569 --> 01:08:41.489
Lauren Infantino: Typically, most of us, I don't think, are going to be doing single fiber electrode readings. Most of us are going to be doing surface EMGs with humans in particular. Some of us will be doing intramuscular EMGs, but surface EMGs and intramuscular EMGs work along the same exact principle.

397
01:08:41.490 --> 01:08:47.790
Lauren Infantino: of basically taking the voltage difference between 2 points as an action potential propagates through a muscle.

398
01:08:48.160 --> 01:08:54.479
Lauren Infantino: Surface EMGs will look at a volume of muscle, though not an individual fiber.

399
01:08:54.840 --> 01:09:06.180
Lauren Infantino: and it'll take a collection of all those motor units that are firing all those action potentials, sum them all up. And this is kind of the output, maybe, that most of us are

400
01:09:06.390 --> 01:09:15.510
Lauren Infantino: have typically seen when we're doing EMG readings, which is somewhat of this noisy summation of a bunch of different action potentials of a muscle.

401
01:09:15.870 --> 01:09:30.110
Lauren Infantino: But really, EMGs will provide us the information about the state of the muscle, so whether it's, like, on or off, how long is it on, and also the magnitude of how much muscle volume is being recruited.

402
01:09:30.550 --> 01:09:45.390
Lauren Infantino: Really, the basis of how bipolar electrodes works kind of inform the standardized practices and principles that we're going to use when we're applying our EMGs onto our subject or inserting our EMGs into a muscle.

403
01:09:46.220 --> 01:09:59.019
Lauren Infantino: The reason that bipolar electrodes really need to be along the length of the muscle is because of the way that they read the difference between 2 points. So let's take the theoretical example.

404
01:09:59.250 --> 01:10:05.659
Lauren Infantino: that let's say the neuromuscular junction is right in between electrode one and electrode 2.

405
01:10:06.000 --> 01:10:11.590
Lauren Infantino: The action potential will propagate in both directions at the same conduction speed.

406
01:10:11.940 --> 01:10:26.640
Lauren Infantino: And because the electrodes band over our neuromuscular junction, the voltage difference between electrode one and electrode two will be zero if they read the action potential at the same exact time.

407
01:10:27.540 --> 01:10:40.000
Lauren Infantino: Another scenario would be if you put electrode one on fiber one. And let's say we have another fiber running along the length of this one. You put electrode 2 on that neighboring fiber.

408
01:10:41.090 --> 01:10:46.260
Lauren Infantino: you'll get an action potential that runs along both fibers at the same conduction speed.

409
01:10:46.370 --> 01:10:52.349
Lauren Infantino: electrode one and electrode 2 will pick up that action potential at the exact same time, and the difference will be 0.

410
01:10:52.690 --> 01:11:00.080
Lauren Infantino: So the basis on how bipolar electrodes work in measuring the voltage difference really informs.

411
01:11:00.150 --> 01:11:16.069
Lauren Infantino: where you're going to put your electrodes on your on your subject, whether they're going to be in parallel with your muscle fibers or not perpendicular with your muscle fibers, and why, if you place it more proximally along the muscle versus more distally along the muscle, you can get completely different readings.

