451
01:17:27.820 --> 01:17:35.570
Lauren Infantino: Cool. So animal models are a little bit different than human models. A lot of the same kind of

452
01:17:35.570 --> 01:17:56.069
Lauren Infantino: principles apply, but because of the amount of maybe more freedom that you have when using an animal model, there's slight differences on maybe some things to keep in mind when you're doing intramuscular EMGs or fine wire EMGs when you're doing small experiments.

453
01:17:56.110 --> 01:17:57.130
Lauren Infantino: So…

454
01:17:57.140 --> 01:18:11.920
Lauren Infantino: For animal models, typically we'll use fine wire electrodes, which are a twisted pair of stainless steel wires with bare ends. These bare ends are basically very synonymous to your electrode receiving ends.

455
01:18:11.920 --> 01:18:24.529
Lauren Infantino: These wires will usually run from the muscle in which it's inserted in, run from the muscle to an external amplifier through a variety of connectors to a DAC.

456
01:18:24.670 --> 01:18:47.480
Lauren Infantino: The main thing about animal models and the placement of the Ems is, you can usually see the muscle. Usually you have the ability to actually physically see the muscle, see the fibers where they're running, what the panation angle is. And so this can provide experimenters a really good idea of exactly where to place the fine wire emgs.

457
01:18:47.580 --> 01:19:10.719
Lauren Infantino: A couple tips that I've got from some postdocs and some PIs at UCI about fine wire electrodes. It's because usually we cannot, stimulate the muscle, and record and verify placement. Really testing your wires before you place them into the animal is probably the most critical step.

458
01:19:10.720 --> 01:19:26.620
Lauren Infantino: You can use a multimeter to do this. Look for conductivity within your wires. Make sure there's no nicks. Make sure the wires are fully insulated other than the bare tips that you're leaving exposed. And when you're inserting, the wires.

459
01:19:26.620 --> 01:19:29.810
Lauren Infantino: usually through a hypodermic needle into the muscle.

460
01:19:30.250 --> 01:19:47.369
Lauren Infantino: Usually, if you press down as you're releasing, so those wires don't come back out or pull out, securing the wires to some skin or some other tissues to help keep them secured, minimize the amount of movement that those wires are undergoing, because that can introduce noise.

461
01:19:47.370 --> 01:19:59.940
Lauren Infantino: and also maintaining a shorter length of the wires. The more length you have, the more areas that there are for noise to get into the system. And so those are kind of the couple of

462
01:20:00.150 --> 01:20:04.629
Lauren Infantino: main things to keep in mind when you're working with animal models and doing emgs.

463
01:20:05.580 --> 01:20:14.499
Lauren Infantino: There are also not just fine wire electrodes that are typically used, but there's other very complex EMG arrays.

464
01:20:14.500 --> 01:20:30.050
Lauren Infantino: In which these can get at, motor unit action potentials. These are pretty high resolution EMGs, so you can see, this is a myomatrix array that was put into the triceps, of a rat.

465
01:20:30.050 --> 01:20:33.750
Lauren Infantino: And it runs along the fibers of the muscle.

466
01:20:33.750 --> 01:20:35.529
Lauren Infantino: And you can see,

467
01:20:35.530 --> 01:21:00.160
Lauren Infantino: comparing figure B, the locomotion from the array and the locomotion from the firewire electrodes, you can really get at the individual motor unit action potentials. So, depending on your question, arrays are a bit better, and they're usually more feasible to do in animal models, which provides a little bit more resolution and depth about activation and motor control, particularly in animals.

468
01:21:01.320 --> 01:21:06.449
Lauren Infantino: But I think Kennedy touched on it. And there's really

469
01:21:06.620 --> 01:21:29.959
Lauren Infantino: most critical step is proper placement, a selection of electrode materials and the configuration. These are the most critical steps of any analyses. Your analyses kind of will be shot if the process of preparing your subject and putting the electrodes in or on the skin isn't done with a lot of care and attention in order to do a good analyses.

470
01:21:30.670 --> 01:21:39.899
Lauren Infantino: proper placement is the most critical part. So really make sure when you're with subjects, human subjects, animal subjects, you're really taking the time to be precise about this part.

471
01:21:41.780 --> 01:21:55.290
Lauren Infantino: So if anyone has any questions before we move on about best practices, how Ems work, we're going to move on next to the signal processing part. But I'll hold for questions if people have any.

472
01:22:03.300 --> 01:22:28.229
Lauren Infantino: Cool. We have a bunch of nervous people, but it's okay. So we'll jump into the processing part of EMGs. So this is kind of going to assume that you've taken the necessary steps to properly prep your subject. You've gotten a pretty nice and clean data set already. And so this is now you have kind of your raw data. And now you're going to go on to processing it to

473
01:22:28.230 --> 01:22:36.559
Lauren Infantino: create your EMG envelope, which is typically what you'll see in the scientific literature when people are showing you their EMG signals from muscles.

474
01:22:36.700 --> 01:22:54.220
Lauren Infantino: So, there are a couple ways that noise can be introduced into the system, and this is stuff that we want to be aware of, and use post-processing algorithms to extract or attenuate and remove. But some common ways that noise can get into an EMG signal

475
01:22:54.220 --> 01:23:14.269
Lauren Infantino: One of them is narrowband noise. This is external noise from the environment. So think of your lights. Think of the power cords, things that are plugged in A/C current. All of these contribute invisible noise which you can't see, and it's really hard to minimize.

476
01:23:14.270 --> 01:23:28.260
Lauren Infantino: The best advice that I've got is unplug things that don't need to be plugged in and combat this with the proper electronic shielding and grounding of all your equipment. This is the greatest way to get rid of that noise.

477
01:23:28.260 --> 01:23:45.809
Lauren Infantino: The second form of noise that's pretty common is biological noise. So this is noise from other tissues. Other tissues are undergoing chemical reactions, they have their own electric fields and potentials, and so this can be picked up by your EMG signal, particularly in surface EMGs that aren't very,

478
01:23:46.080 --> 01:23:56.840
Lauren Infantino: aren't very aren't directly on the muscle that they're measuring. These are typically lower frequencies. And you'll usually just post process this signal out.

479
01:23:57.750 --> 01:24:04.609
Lauren Infantino: This 3rd one is going to be movement artifacts. So you're you're using a surface. Emg, for example.

480
01:24:05.510 --> 01:24:15.980
Lauren Infantino: The hardware itself can vibrate. That vibration or that motion can be detected, picked up by the surface EMG itself.

