01:24:15.980 --> 01:24:22.489
Lauren Infantino: It's typically high-frequency components. These are also passed, can be post-processed out.

482
01:24:22.490 --> 01:24:39.240
Lauren Infantino: The main thing that people should take away is that post-processing algorithms are really the way to, take your raw signal into the envelope signal. It can eliminate any extraneous noise that you have and really capture what the underlying muscle is actually doing.

483
01:24:39.380 --> 01:24:51.090
Lauren Infantino: And so it's really important to design your algorithm and your filtering process in kind of the correct way, and to always be double checking at every step through that.

484
01:24:52.770 --> 01:25:08.810
Lauren Infantino: So a little bit about fast Fourier transformation. So this is a kind of more of a complex topic. But it's actually pretty simple in in just its totality. But let's say you have a raw EMG signal.

485
01:25:08.900 --> 01:25:12.909
Lauren Infantino: So along the time domain, meaning the time is on the x-axis.

486
01:25:13.320 --> 01:25:21.999
Lauren Infantino: The fast Fourier transformation will basically take your raw signal, break it down into the various frequency components.

487
01:25:22.110 --> 01:25:34.669
Lauren Infantino: and provide you an EMG power spectrum, which is just basically the frequencies that are contained within the signal, and how much of their frequencies you have, which is the magnitude.

488
01:25:35.220 --> 01:25:54.520
Lauren Infantino: This is a good way to initially, if you're coming in and you have raw EMG and you don't really know how to design your filtering algorithm, it's a really good way to — it's a really good point to start here. Take the fast Fourier transformation of your raw EMG signal. Look at the frequency components that are within your signal.

489
01:25:54.520 --> 01:25:58.950
Lauren Infantino: and then kind of deduce what you should be filtering in and out.

490
01:25:58.950 --> 01:26:17.740
Lauren Infantino: This will give you a good indication if you have too much high frequency noise coming from external sources. You have that narrow band noise at 50 hertz. So this is a really good tool to use. You can implement this in various computational programs like MATLAB, Python, etc.

491
01:26:19.840 --> 01:26:21.050
Lauren Infantino: So…

492
01:26:21.200 --> 01:26:34.509
Lauren Infantino: filters. So this is usually what will be within your post processing algorithm is usually a Butterworth filter. These consist of low pass filters, high pass filters, bandpass filters, and notch filters.

493
01:26:34.510 --> 01:26:43.039
Lauren Infantino: So for all the images on panel A, these are all ffts or fast Fourier transformations.

494
01:26:43.040 --> 01:26:48.799
Lauren Infantino: which have frequency on the X, and then the magnitude of that signal on the Y.

495
01:26:48.800 --> 01:26:55.079
Lauren Infantino: So a low pass filter is a filter in which I kind of think of pass low.

496
01:26:55.280 --> 01:27:11.510
Lauren Infantino: So we're going to take our cutoff frequency, which is the frequency that we want to tell our filter, hey, don't take anything beyond this point. So for example, this one in panel A for low pass is, let's say, 180 hertz.

